{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2020",
    "lib": ["dom", "dom.iterable", "esnext"],
    "allowJs": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "module": "esnext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "jsx": "react-jsx",
    "incremental": true,
    "plugins": [{ "name": "next" }],
    "paths": { "@/*": ["./src/*"] }
  },
  // Next writes both type globs into `include` itself (and puts them back if
  // they are removed), so they stay: `.next/types/**` is what `next build`
  // emits, and it carries the typed-route helpers (PageProps, LayoutProps,
  // RouteContext, ParamMap) plus the per-route validator that checks every
  // page/layout/handler's exported shape. That check is worth keeping.
  "include": ["next-env.d.ts", "**/*.ts", "**/*.tsx", ".next/types/**/*.ts", ".next/dev/types/**/*.ts"],
  // `.next/dev/types` is the SAME thing regenerated continuously by `next dev`,
  // and it is excluded here for exactly the reason Next gives for filtering it
  // out of its own build type check. Next's words, in
  // next/dist/lib/typescript/runTypeCheck.js:
  //
  //   tsconfig includes both .next/types and .next/dev/types to avoid config
  //   churn between dev/build modes. During build, we filter out
  //   .next/dev/types files to prevent stale dev types from causing errors
  //   when routes have been deleted since the last dev session.
  //
  // `next build` therefore never compiles that directory (getDevTypesPath in
  // type-paths.js returns it, and runTypeCheck drops every fileName under it),
  // while a plain `tsc --noEmit` had no such filter and compiled whatever the
  // dev server happened to have half-written. This exclude gives `npm run
  // typecheck` the same file list the build already uses, so the two agree.
  // It does not fight Next either: writeConfigurationDefaults only ever ADDS
  // to `include`, and only seeds `exclude` when the key is missing.
  //
  // Nothing is lost. `.next/types` stays in `include` and is the copy that
  // matters: `next build` writes it fresh and then checks it, and it carries
  // both the typed-route helpers (PageProps, LayoutProps, RouteContext,
  // ParamMap) and the per-route validator that checks every
  // page/layout/handler's exported shape. The generated next-env.d.ts imports
  // ./.next/types/routes.d.ts and ./.next/types/root-params.d.ts by path, from
  // that same non-excluded directory, so the route helper types reach the
  // program in dev as well. What drops out is only the dev server's duplicate,
  // above all its validator.ts, which is rewritten on every compile and points
  // at route modules a stale .next no longer has.
  "exclude": ["node_modules", ".next/dev/types"]
}
